Integer coefficients for the element routines in this addon.


Shape functions/interpolations
------------------------------

See file addons/README_basefunctions


Numerical integration
---------------------

See file addons/README_Gauss


List of the coefficients
------------------------

1   interpolation of the velocity at the interface
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral interpolation also the polynomial order p
          needs to be set with coefficients%i(2).

2   Polynomial order p of the spectral/high-order interpolation for the
    velocity

3   Numerical integration rule for the internal domain integrals of elements.

4   nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(3) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by
        coefficients%i(3) to each subdomain.

5   Type of integration (inttype):
      0: standard Gauss-Legendre (analytical expressions)
      1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
      2: mapped Gauss (quad->triangle).
      3: standard Gauss-Legendre (numerical tables)

6   secondary numerical integration rule (intrule2) for the internal domain
    integrals of elements. This is only relevant for prisms and pyramids

7   Specifies how the integration rules must be set:
    0: default, i.e. direcly with the value in coefficients.
    1: by specifying the minimal polynomial order that the rule must be able
       to integrate exactly.
    Note, that the value of coefficients%i(77) is overruled by setting
    SET_GAUSS_BY_ORDER = .true. in the module limits_m.

8   coordinate system for 2D elements:
    0: Cartesian planar (x,y)
    1: Cylindrical (z,r), axisymmetrical
    Note that for cylindrical/axisymmetrical coordinates we have (z,r),
    i.e. the first coordinate is the axial coordinate z and the second
    coordinate is the radial coordinate r. The third coordinate is theta,
    but is not needed explicitly in an axisymmetrical flow.

9   Use a 3D velocity vector in a 2D space:
      0: no
      1: yes
    NOTE: the definition of the third velocity component depends on the
          coordinate system used (coefficients%i(23)):
     * velocity component u_z in the 2D Cartesian system (coefficients%i(23)=0).
     * circumferential velocity component u_theta in the axisymmetrical case,
       i.e. add "swirl". (coefficients%i(23)=1).
    NOTE: Not all elements have been modified to take this into account.
          An error message is given if incompatible elements are called.
    NOTE: Not all add-ons have implemented the third component. Hence, check
          the elements before using coefficients%i(67)=1.

10  vfuncnr for the vector u (=velocity)

11  interfacial rheological model number:
    0: surface tension only
    1: Boussinesq-Scriven (viscous)
    2: Verwijlen (elastic)
    3: Kelvin-Voigt 1 (model 1+2)
    4: Huetter-Tervoort (elastic)
    5: Kelvin-Voigt 2 (model 1+4)

12  surface tension semi-implicit or explicit in momentum balance
    0: explicit
    1: semi-implicit

13  pchoice for plotting quantities on the interface
                            __
    1: surface divergence = \/ . u
                              s
    2: surface rate of strain = sqrt ( 2Ds:Ds )
                            __
    3: surface vorticity = (\/ x u) . n

    4: surface determinant of Fs

14--100 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

